hadiths[35]{hadithnumber,text}:
"956","The Prophet (ﷺ) set out on Thursday for the expedition of Tabuk. He liked to set out on a journey on Thursdays. [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]"
"957","On the authority of Sakhr bin Wada’a al-Ghamdi, the companion, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “O Allah, bless my nation in its early mornings (at dawn).” And if he sent a company or an army, he would send them from the beginning of the day. Sakhr was a merchant, so he would send out his trade at the beginning of the day, so he became wealthy and his money increased. [Narrated by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi, who said: a good hadith]."
"958","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, Were people to know of what I know about the dangers of travelling alone"","" no rider would travel alone at night.\"" [Al-Bukhari] .""
"959","On the authority of Omar bin Shuaib, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “The rider is a devil, the two riders are two devils, and the three are riders” [Narrated by Abu Dawud, Al-Tirmidhi, and Al-Nasa’i with authentic chains of transmission, and Al-Tirmidhi said: A good hadith]"
"960","On the authority of Abu Saeed and Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with them, they said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “If three people go out on a journey, let them appoint one of them as their leader (amir).” A good hadith, [Abu Dawud]."
"961","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ) he said: “The best companions are four, the best companies are four hundred, and the best armies are four thousand, and twelve thousand will not be defeated by a small number” [Abu Dawud and Al-Tirmidhi]"
"962","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: When you travel through green and lush land"","" give the camels their due from the ground"","" and when you travel through a dry and barren land"","" make them walk quickly lest their strength falters. When you encamp at night"","" keep away from the roads"","" for beasts pass there and they are the resort of insects at night.\"" [Muslim] .""
"963","When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) encamped at night during a journey, he would lie down on his right side, and when he stayed a little before dawn he would raise his forearm and place his head on the palm of his hand. [Muslim] ."
"964","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, Keep to travelling by night because the earth is folded (traversed more easily) during the night.\"" [Abu Dawud] .""
"965","On the authority of Abu Tha’labah al-Khushani, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When people camped in a camp, they would disperse through the paths and valleys. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “If you disperse in these paths and valleys, that is only from Satan!” They did not camp after that until they joined each other. ([Abu Dawud] Hassan)."
"966","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) happened to pass by a camel whose belly was sticking to its back (because of hunger), whereupon he said, Fear Allah in respect of these dumb (animals). Ride them while they are fit"","" and slaughter them and eat their meat when they are fit.\"" [Abu Dawud] .""
"967","On the authority of Abu Jaafar Abdullah bin Jaafar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led me back one day behind him, and he confided to me a conversation that I would not narrate to any of the people. The most beloved thing that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to cover himself with for his need was a goal or a palm tree. That is, a wall of palm trees. [It was narrated like this) Briefly.) Al-Barqani added to it with the chain of transmission of Muslim after his saying: A tree of palm trees: So he entered the wall of a man from the Ansar, and there was a camel in it. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw it grunted and its eyes shed tears, the Prophet (ﷺ) and (ﷺ) came to him and wiped its back - that is, its hump - and its dhafarah - and it calmed down, and he said: Who is the owner of this camel? Who is this camel for? Then a young man from the Ansar came and said: This is for me, O Messenger of Allah. Then he said: Do you not fear Allah regarding this animal that Allah has given you? For he complains to me that you starve him and tire him out ((Narrated by Abu Dawud as narrated by Al-Barqani]. His saying: Dhafarah, which is made with a kasra on the dhaal and a fa’, and it is a singular, feminine word. The linguists said: Al-Dhafri: the place where the camel sweats behind the ear, and his saying: “Tathabaha” means: “You tire him out.”"
"968","And on the authority of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When we camped, we would not perform the tasbih until we had left the mount. [Narrated by Abu Dawud with a chain of transmission according to the conditions of Muslim]. And his saying: We do not perform the tasbih, meaning we do not pray the voluntary prayer, and its meaning is: I - despite our keenness to pray - do not put it before the mount or rest the animals."
"969","On the authority of Abu Saeed Al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: While we were on a journey, a man came upon a camel of his, and he began to look right and left, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Whoever has an extra surplus, let him return it to the one who does not have it, and whoever has an extra surplus, let him return it to the one who has no provision.” So he mentioned the types of money that he mentioned, until we saw: that none of us has the right to Fadl” [Muslim]."
"970","When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made up his mind to go in an expedition, he would say to us, O Muhajirin and the Ansar! There are among you such people who have no property and no kinsfolk. Let everyone of you take along with him two or three men. None of us had a spare animal we took rides by turn.\"" So I took two or three men with me and I rode my camel by turn equally with them. [Abu Dawud] .""
"971","On his authority, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to stay behind in the march, so he would push the weak person forward and let him ride behind him and pray for him. [Abu Dawud]."
"972","Whenever the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mounted his camel for setting out on a journey, he would recite: Allahu Akbar (Allah is Greatest)"",""\"" thrice. Then he (ﷺ) would supplicate: \""Subhanal-ladhi sakh-khara lana hadha"","" wa ma kunna lahu muqrinin"","" wa inna ila Rabbina lamunqalibun. Allahumma inna nas'aluka fi safarina hadh al-birra wat-taqwa"","" wa minal-'amali ma tarda. Allahumma hawwin 'alaina safarana hadha"","" watwi 'anna bu'dahu. Allahumma Antas-Sahibu fissafari"","" wal-Khalifatu fil- ahli. Allahumma inni a'udhu bika min wa'ta'is-safari"","" wa kaabatil- manzari"","" wa su'il-munqalabi fil-mali wal-ahli wal-waladi (Far removed from imperfection is the One Who has made this subservient to us"","" for we have no power to subjugate it"","" and certainly to our Rubb shall we return. O Allah"","" we ask You during this journey of ours for righteousness"","" piety and such deeds as are pleasing to You. O Allah"","" make easy for us this journey of ours and make the distance short for us. O Allah"","" You are our Companion during the journey and the Guardian of the family and the property in our absence. O Allah"","" I seek refuge in You from the hardships of travelling"","" unhappiness connected with ghastly scenes and evil turns in property and family).\"" When he returned"","" he recited this supplication making addition of these words: \""Ayibuna"","" ta'ibuna"","" 'abiduna"","" li-Rabbina hamidun (We are those who return; those who repent; those who worship and those who praise our Rubb).\"" [Muslim] .""
"973","On the authority of Abdullah bin Sarjis, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Whenever the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) traveled, he would seek refuge from the hardships of travel, the gloom of a turning point, the alteration after stability, the supplication of the oppressed, and the bad sight of one’s family and money. [Muslim] This is how it is in Sahih Muslim: The houris after the universe, with the letter nun, and so it was narrated by Al-Tirmidhi and Al-Nasa’i. He said. Al-Tirmidhi: He narrates: Al-Kur is pronounced with the Ra’, and both of them have a face. The scholars said: The meaning of the Nun and the Ra’ together is: returning from straightness or increase to deficiency. They said: The narration of the Ra’ is taken from twisting the turban, which is to wrap and gather it, and the narration of the Nun, from the universe, a source that would have been a universe if it existed and was stable."
"974","On the authority of Ali bin Rabi’ah, he said: I witnessed Ali bin Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, when an animal was brought to him to ride. When he put his foot in the stirrup, he said: In the name of Allah. When he was leveled on its back, he said: Praise be to Allah who has subjected this to us, and we were not able to subdue it, and to our Lord we will return. Then he said: Praise be to Allah, three times, then he said: Allah is Great, three times, then he said: Glory be to You, I have wronged myself, so forgive me. for none forgives sins except You. Then he laughed, and it was said: O Commander of the Faithful, at what did you laugh? He said: I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) do as I did, then he laughed, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, at what did you laugh? He said: “Your Lord, Glory be to Him, admires His servant when he says: Forgive me my sins, knowing that no one else forgives sins.” (Narrated by: Abu Dawud and Al-Tirmidhi said: It is a hasan hadith. And in some versions: it is an authentic hadith. This is the wording of Abu Dawud."
"975","On the authority of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When we ascended, we would say “Allahu Akbar,” and when we descended, we would say “Glory be to Him.” [Al-Bukhari]."
"976","On the authority of Ibn Omar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) and his armies, when they ascended the heights, would say “Allahu Akbar,” and when they descended, they would say “Glory be to Him (SubhanAllah).” ([Abu Dawud]"
"977","Whenever the Prophet (ﷺ) returned from Hajj or 'Umrah and ascended a height or hill, he would recite thrice: Allahu Akbar (Allah is Greatest).\"" Then he would say"","" \""La ilaha ill-allahu wahdahu la sharika lahu"","" lahul- mulku"","" wa lahul-hamdu"","" wa Huwa 'ala kulli shay'in Qadeer. Ayibuna ta'ibuna 'abiduna li-Rabbina hamidun. Sadaq-Allahu wa'dahu"","" wa nasara 'abdahu"","" wa hazamal-ahzaba wahdahu [None has the right to be worshipped but Allah"","" Who has no partner"","" to Whom the sovereignty belongs"","" to Whom all praise is due"","" and Who is Able to do all things. We are those who return (in safety)"","" feel penitence"","" worship Allah"","" prostrate before our Rubb and praise Him. Allah has fulfilled His Promise"","" helped His slave (i.e."","" Muhammad (ﷺ)) and He Alone vanquished the Clans] .\"" [Al-Bukhari and Muslim] .""
"978","And on the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I want to travel, so advise me. He said: “You must fear Allah and glorify Allah at every elevated place.” When the man turned away, he said: “O Allah, lengthen the distance for him, and make the journey easy for him” [Al-Tirmidhi]."
"979","On the authority of Abu Musa Al-Ash’ari, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Prophet (ﷺ) on a journey. When we approached a valley, we would declare the oneness of Allah (say La ilaha illAllah) and say “Allahu Akbar” and our voices would rise, so the Prophet (ﷺ) said: “O people, be careful with yourselves, for you will not call the deaf or the absent. He is with you. He is the Hearer and Near.” (Agreed upon) “Arba`a” with the opening of the unified ba’, meaning: be kind to yourselves."
"980","On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Three supplications are answered, of which there is no doubt: the supplication of the oppressed, the supplication of the traveler, and the supplication of the father for his child” [Abu Dawud and Al-Tirmidhi] and not in Abu Dawud’s narration: “for his son.”"
"981","On the authority of Abu Musa Al-Ash’ari, may Allah be pleased with him, that when the Messenger of Allah feared a people, he would say: “O Allah, we place You in their throats, and we seek refuge in You from their evils” [Narrated by Abu Dawud, and Al-Nasa’i with an authentic chain of transmission]."
"982","I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying, Whosoever alights somewhere and says: 'A'udhu bikalimat-illahit-tammati min sharri ma khalaqa (I seek refuge with the Perfect Words of Allah from the evil of what He has created)"",""' nothing will harm him until he leaves that place.\"" [Muslim] .""
"983","On the authority of Ibn Omar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) traveled and night fell, he would say: “O earth, my Lord and your Lord is Allah. I seek refuge in Allah from your polytheism and the evil of what is within you, the evil of what He has created in you, and the evil of what creeps upon you. I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of the lion and the person, from the serpent and from the scorpion, from the inhabitant of the land, and from Satan and the devils” ([Abu Dawud] and the black: the person. Al-Khattabi said: And the inhabitant of the town: they are the jinn who are the inhabitants of the earth. He said: And the town is from the earth: what was the shelter of animals, even if there were no buildings or houses in it. He said: It is possible that what is meant by father is: Satan, and what is born: devils."
"984","On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Travel is a piece of torment. It deprives one of you of his food, drink, and sleep. If one of you fulfills his purpose from his travel, let him hasten to his family.” [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]. His hunger: his purpose."
"985","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, When one of you stays away (from his family) for a long period of time"","" let him not surprise his family by night.\"" In another narration"","" Jabir (May Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited us from coming back to our family at night. [Al-Bukhari and Muslim] .""
"986","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would not come (back) to his family by night. He used to come in the morning or in the evening (before dark). [Al-Bukhari and Muslim] ."
"987","On the authority of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We came with the Prophet (ﷺ) until when we were at the back of Medina, he said: “Returning, repentant, worshipers, praising our Lord.” He continued to say that until we arrived at Medina, [Muslim]."
"988","On the authority of Ka’b bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came from a journey, he would start at the mosque and perform two rak’ahs in it. [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]."
"989","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, It is not permissible for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to make a journey of one day and night unless she is accompanied by a Mahram (husband or any other relative to whom she is prohibited to marry).\"" [Al-Bukhari and Muslim] .""
"990","And on the authority of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: “A man should not be alone with a woman unless she has a mahram with her, and a woman should not travel except with a mahram.” Then a man said to him: O Messenger of Allah, my wife went out on a pilgrimage, and I was registered for such-and-such expedition? He said: “Go and perform Hajj with your wife.” [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]."
